page ref_vm {
  title = "Lab file: vm and its children"
  chapter_header "Lab file: vm and its children" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "18 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-30"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    This chapter is the field-by-field reference for the `vm {}` block and
    every block it can carry. The blocks a `vm` shares with a `container`
    (`login`, `nic`, `provision`, `playbook`) are documented here once and
    referenced from [container and its children](ref_container). How a VM's
    hardware resolves is explained in the [lab file guide](guide_lab_file);
    what the schema does not say is enforced by [vmlab validate](cmd_validate),
    and each entry below lists those rules.
  DOC

  h2 "vm {}"
  p <<DOC
    One virtual machine. The only required field is `template`; every
    hardware value not set here is inherited from the template's recorded
    hardware, then from the [guest OS profile](guide_profiles).
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
vm "<name>" {
  template    = "<arch>/<name>[@<version>][#<variant>]"   // or "scratch", or a registry ref
  arch        = "x86_64"
  profile     = "linux-modern"
  cpus        = 2
  memory      = 4GiB
  disk        = 64GiB          // scratch VMs only
  cdrom       = "./isos/boot.iso"
  floppy      = "./boot.img"
  depends_on  = ["dc01"]
  depends_on_provision = ["dc01"]
  nested      = false
  gui         = false
  agent_update = true
  prevent_sleep = false
  clock       = "host"         // or "snapshot", or "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"
  display     = "virtio-vga"
  cpu_model   = "pentium3"
  hyperv      = true
  apple_smc   = false
  input       = "ps2"          // or "usb"
  tsc         = "host"
  accel       = "tcg"
  firmware    = "ovmf"
  tpm         = false
  audio       = false
  microphone  = false
  secure_boot = false
  qemu_args   = []
  hypervisor  = "qemu"
  gpu       { … }
  nic       { … }
  disk "…"  { … }
  share     { … }
  media     { … }
  login "…" { … }
  provision "…" { … }
  playbook "…" { … }
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "VM name, a DNS label, unique per lab; the inline block label." |
      | "`template`" | "utf8" | "required" | "`<arch>/<name>[@<version>][#<variant>]` from the store, `scratch`, or an OCI registry reference with an optional `#<variant>`." |
      | "`arch`" | "utf8" | "from template" | "Architecture. Required for `scratch` and for registry references." |
      | "`profile`" | "utf8" | "from template" | "Guest OS profile supplying hardware defaults. Required for `scratch`." |
      | "`cpus`" | "i64" | "inherited" | "vCPU count, greater than 0. Inherited from template, then profile, if omitted." |
      | "`memory`" | "ByteSize" | "inherited" | "RAM as a byte size, for example `8GiB` or `512MiB`. Inherited if omitted." |
      | "`disk`" | "ByteSize" | "none" | "Primary disk size, for example `64GiB`. Scratch VMs only; rejected on cloned VMs." |
      | "`cdrom`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Path to an ISO to attach as a CD-ROM, relative to the lab root." |
      | "`floppy`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Path to a floppy image to attach, relative to the lab root." |
      | "`depends_on`" | "list<utf8>" | "none" | "Start order: VM or container names that must be ready before this one starts. Ready means a VM's agent answers or a container passes its healthcheck; their provisioning is not waited for. `down` stops in reverse. No self-reference, no cycles." |
      | "`depends_on_provision`" | "list<utf8>" | "none" | "Provisioning order: VM or container names whose provision and playbook steps must all finish before this VM's first step runs. Does not delay this VM's start. No self-reference, no cycles." |
      | "`nested`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Expose hardware virtualisation (VMX/SVM) to the guest. Without it, an x86 guest under KVM has both masked. `up` warns when the host's KVM module has nested virtualisation off. No effect under TCG or on other architectures." |
      | "`gui`" | "bool" | "lab `gui`" | "Open a VNC viewer on `up`. The VM always runs headless." |
      | "`agent_update`" | "bool" | "lab `agent_update`, else `true`" | "Refresh the guest agent on `up` and `vm start` when its version stamp differs from the agent this vmlab ships, and mark the VM diverged. `false` leaves the agent the template sealed. See [vmlab up](cmd_up)." |
      | "`prevent_sleep`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Keep the guest awake: the moment it suspends to RAM (ACPI S3), the lab daemon wakes it with QMP `system_wakeup` and records `vm.woken` with `cause = prevent_sleep`. Declared here only, never inherited: it is the daemon's policy, not hardware. The template build VM always has it on. Not accepted on a `container {}`. See [vmlab status](cmd_status)." |
      | "`clock`" | "utf8" | "`host`" | "Guest clock policy. `host`: the RTC runs from the host's time, the lab daemon checks the guest's clock at every agent handshake and sets it when it is more than 2 s off, and after an online snapshot restore it sets the guest's clock to the host's current time through the agent. `snapshot`: a restored guest keeps the time it was captured at. An RFC 3339 date-time such as `2020-01-01T00:00:00Z` (any offset, converted to UTC): the RTC starts at that instant on every boot and runs forward, and vmlab never sets it, after a restore included. Declared here only, never inherited. The RTC holds UTC unless the profile sets `rtc = \"localtime\"`, which starts it at the host's local time instead. See [Snapshots](guide_snapshots)." |
      | "`display`" | "utf8" | "inherited" | "Display device: `virtio-vga`, `virtio-gpu`, `qxl`, `std`, `vmware` (VMware SVGA II, `vmware-svga`, which macOS drives with no driver of its own), or a QEMU device name passed as written. Inherited from template, then profile." |
      | "`cpu_model`" | "utf8" | "inherited" | "QEMU `-cpu` model, for example `pentium3`, `pentium2`, `qemu32` or `Westmere`, replacing the default (`host` under KVM, `max` under TCG) under either accelerator. For guests the host CPU breaks, such as Windows 9x Setup. Inherited from template, then profile. Any non-empty name is accepted; QEMU refuses an unknown one at start. Cannot be combined with `nested = true`." |
      | "`hyperv`" | "bool" | "inherited" | "Hyper-V enlightenments for a Windows guest: under KVM on x86 the `-cpu host` string gains `hv-relaxed`, `hv-vapic`, `hv-spinlocks=0x1fff`, `hv-vpindex`, `hv-runtime`, `hv-time`, `hv-synic`, `hv-stimer`, `hv-frequencies`, `hv-reset`, `hv-tlbflush` and `hv-ipi`, so Windows reads its clock from the Hyper-V reference TSC page instead of an emulated timer and takes fewer exits for interrupts, timers, TLB flushes and IPIs. Inherited from template, then profile; the shipped `windows-11`, `windows-10`, `windows-server` and `windows-legacy` profiles set it, so `false` here opts a VM out. Not applied under TCG, with a `cpu_model`, or on a non-x86 guest; `up` prints a warning when this block set `true` and it was not applied. Where the host's KVM has APICv or AVIC on, `hv-avic` is added too, with `-global kvm-pit.lost_tick_policy=discard`, so interrupts are acknowledged without an exit. Online snapshots keep working: `+invtsc`, which blocks migration, is never added." |
      | "`apple_smc`" | "bool" | "inherited" | "The Apple SMC a macOS guest refuses to boot without: adds `-device isa-applesmc,osk=…` with the host-supplied `apple_smc_osk` and `-smbios type=2`. The key must come from your own Apple hardware; see [Host configuration](ref_host). Inherited from template, then profile; the shipped `macos` profile sets it. Off when no layer says. x86 only: a VM of another arch that resolves it is refused, naming the layer." |
      | "`input`" | "utf8" | "inherited, else `ps2`" | "Keyboard and pointer. `ps2`: the PS/2 keyboard and a `usb-tablet` on `-usb`. `usb`: a `usb-kbd` and a `usb-tablet` on an xHCI of their own (`qemu-xhci,id=xhci`, no `-usb`), for a guest that reads no PS/2 keyboard, such as macOS and OpenCore; on a `virt` machine the keyboard joins the tablet's xHCI. Neither is bound to the display, so QMP `send-key` reaches the USB keyboard and `send_keys` works with the default `input_transport`. Inherited from template, then profile; the `macos` profile sets `usb`." |
      | "`tsc`" | "utf8" | "inherited" | "`host`: an invariant TSC pinned at the host's frequency, `,+invtsc,tsc-frequency=<Hz>` on `-cpu`, which macOS needs. The frequency is read from KVM at the VM's first KVM start and pinned in `.vmlab/vms/<vm>/tsc-hz`; later starts reuse it, so online snapshots keep restoring after the host recalibrates its TSC at a reboot. Applied under KVM only: under TCG `up` warns and the VM boots with QEMU's default TSC. x86 only. Inherited from template, then profile; the `macos` profile sets it. Unset is QEMU's default." |
      | "`accel`" | "utf8" | "inherited" | "Accelerator: `kvm` or `tcg`. Unset, KVM when usable, else TCG with a warning. `tcg` always emulates, even where KVM works, for guests KVM cannot run reliably, such as Windows 95 on an AMD host; `-cpu` stays `max` unless `cpu_model` names one. `kvm` refuses to start the VM when KVM is unusable rather than falling back. Inherited from template, then profile. `tcg` cannot be combined with `nested = true`." |
      | "`firmware`" | "utf8" | "inherited" | "Firmware: `ovmf` or `seabios`. Inherited from template, then profile." |
      | "`tpm`" | "bool" | "inherited" | "Enable a TPM 2.0 device. Inherited from template, then profile." |
      | "`audio`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "A sound card (Intel HDA) whose playback is the host audio node `vmlab.<lab>.<machine>`. The VM's own; never inherited. Needs `hypervisor = \"vmlab\"`. See [Guest audio](guide_audio)." |
      | "`microphone`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "The host's default microphone as the sound card's capture stream. Needs `audio = true` and `hypervisor = \"vmlab\"`." |
      | "`usb`" | "bool" | "inherited" | "Boot with a hot-plug xHCI controller so [vmlab usb](cmd_usb) can pass host USB devices in. Inherited from the profile only — a template has no say; off when neither sets it. Changes the VM's hardware, so online snapshots taken without it no longer restore." |
      | "`secure_boot`" | "bool" | "inherited" | "Enable secure boot; OVMF only. Inherited from template, then profile." |
      | "`secure_boot_keys`" | "utf8" | "inherited, else `microsoft`" | "The key store secure boot starts from. `microsoft`: the bundled store with Microsoft's keys enrolled. `setup`: the blank store, so the firmware starts in setup mode and the guest enrolls its own keys on first boot; the VM's `OVMF_VARS.fd` keeps them. Inherited from template, then profile. Needs `secure_boot = true`. See [Host and firmware](guide_host)." |
      | "`secure_boot_vars`" | "utf8" | "none" | "A pre-enrolled UEFI VARS file, relative to the lab root, copied once at the VM's first start in place of the bundled store, so the guest enforces your keys from its first boot. Must be the size of the secure-boot firmware's VARS template. Needs `secure_boot = true`; exclusive with `secure_boot_keys`. Overrides a template's stored VARS." |
      | "`qemu_args`" | "list<utf8>" | "none" | "Raw QEMU flags appended last. The escape hatch; they win over everything vmlab generates. Refused on any other hypervisor." |
      | "`hypervisor`" | "utf8" | "host default, else `qemu`" | "The program that runs the VM: `qemu`, or `vmlab` for vmlab-hv. vmlab-hv supports modern q35 VMs through phases 2a to 2f. Legacy pc support is in progress: 4a machine core and IDE, 4b e1000 and PCnet, 4c floppy and UHCI, 4d Cirrus, and 4e vmlab integration. Undelivered hardware is refused by sub-phase. Unset takes the host config's default for VMs. Declared on the VM only; it never resolves through template or profile. See [QEMU or vmlab-hv](guide_host)." |
      | "`gpu {}`" | "child" | "none" | "GPU acceleration: passthrough, virgl or vulkan." |
      | "`nic {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Network interfaces. No NICs means air-gapped. Shares need at least one." |
      | "`disk {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Additional disks beyond the primary disk." |
      | "`share {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Shared folders over virtiofs or SMB. SMB shares require at least one NIC." |
      | "`media {}`" | "children" | "none" | "ISO or floppy images built from a folder." |
      | "`login {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Identities a person's commands run as on this VM. Without one every verb keeps the agent identity." |
      | "`provision {}`" | "children" | "none" | "wscript scripts run on `vmlab up` once this VM is ready, interleaved with its playbooks in declaration order." |
      | "`playbook {}`" | "children" | "none" | "config-weave playbooks applied on `vmlab up`, interleaved with its provisions in declaration order." |
  }
  h3 "Template references"
  p "The `template` value takes one of three forms, and the form decides what else the block must say."
  list {
    li "**Store reference** `<arch>/<name>[@<version>][#<variant>]`, such as `x86_64/windows-11@26100.1` or `x86_64/windows-11#win3d`. The arch is always written; a bare `windows-11` is rejected. Without a version the highest version in the store is used. The template must be in the store, and an `arch` field, if present, must match."
    li "**Registry reference**, such as `ghcr.io/owner/ubuntu-24.04:1.0`, with an optional `#<variant>` after the tag or digest. Recognised by a first path segment holding a dot, a colon or `localhost`. It requires an explicit `arch`. `vmlab up` pulls it if it is absent from the store and never re-pulls it implicitly. See [Distributing templates over registries](guide_registries)."
    li "**`scratch`**: no backing image. The VM gets a blank disk and no template layer. It requires `arch`, `profile` and `disk`, and boot media is yours to attach."
  }
  p "Known architectures are `x86_64`, `x86`, `aarch64`, `riscv64`, `loongarch64`, `s390x` and `ppc64`."
  p <<DOC
    The `#<variant>` suffix selects one disk of a template version that has
    variants. Without it the template's default variant is used, or its only
    disk when it has none. A variant name is 1 to 32 lowercase letters,
    digits and hyphens, starting with a letter or digit. `#base` names the
    unsealed base disk and is refused on a template that has variants. The
    rules are in [Templates](guide_templates).
  DOC
  h3 "Validation"
  list {
    li "The name is a DNS label and no other VM or container in the lab has it."
    li "`cpus` is at least 1. `memory` and `disk` are non-negative sizes."
    li "`disk` on a store or registry template is an error; clones inherit the template's disk. Use `disk \"name\" {}` blocks for extra disks."
    li "A `#<variant>` names a variant the store template has, and is not `#base` on a template with variants. The error lists the template's variants, the default marked."
    li "Every name in `depends_on` and `depends_on_provision` exists in the lab and is not the VM itself."
    li "Neither key forms a cycle across VMs and containers; the error names the whole path. The two keys are separate graphs, so a loop that crosses from one to the other is allowed: starting a machine never waits for provisioning, so it cannot deadlock."
    li "`profile`, if set, names a known profile."
    li "A VM on `hypervisor = \"vmlab\"`, declared or from the host default, is refused anything vmlab-hv cannot run yet, one line per reason naming the phase that brings it: a macOS guest; the i440fx machine, a floppy or `agent_transport = \"isa-serial\"` (4); `accel = \"tcg\"` (3); a display device other than std VGA or virtio-vga, a NIC model other than virtio-net, a `cpu_model`, a pinned `tsc` or `input = \"usb\"`; and `qemu_args`, in every phase. Firmware, display and machine usually come from the template, so validation reads them where the template is in the store, and `up` checks again."
    li "`audio = true` or `microphone = true` on a VM that runs on QEMU is refused, naming vmlab-hv; `microphone = true` without `audio = true` is refused on either."
    li "`cdrom` and `floppy` name files that exist under the lab root."
    li "`clock` is `host`, `snapshot`, or an RFC 3339 date-time such as `2020-01-01T00:00:00Z`."
    li "`secure_boot = true` is rejected when the firmware resolves to `seabios`. Either value may have been inherited, so the message names the layer each came from."
    li "`secure_boot_keys` (other than `microsoft`) or `secure_boot_vars` is rejected where secure boot resolves off, naming the layer each side came from. A key store inherited from below the layer that turned secure boot off is not an error. The two are rejected together on one block, and `secure_boot_vars` must name an existing file."
    li "A VM with `share {}` blocks that are not `transport = \"virtiofs\"` needs at least one `nic {}`, because SMB is reachable only over a segment."
    li "A `playbook {}` on a VM whose arch is known to be something other than `x86_64` is rejected unless it names a `guest_bin`; config-weave ships guest binaries for x86_64 only."
    li "A `playbook {}` on a macOS guest (a profile named `macos…`) is rejected unless it names a `guest_bin`; config-weave ships no macOS binary."
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/mixed-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/mixed-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    anchor      = "vm-winsrv"
    dedent      = true
  }

  h3 "The @dev decorator"
  p <<DOC
    `@dev` is written on the line before a `vm` or `container` block and marks
    it as a [dev machine](guide_dev): when a workspace is named, vmlab syncs
    that directory onto it and keeps the two copies in step. It is a decorator rather than a child block because it states
    something about the machine; nothing it carries is a setting the guest
    sees. A bare `@dev` is complete. Any number of machines may carry it, and
    zero is normal.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
@dev(default = true, workspace = "./workspace", workspace_guest = "C:\\src")
vm "dev01" { … }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Argument" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`default`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Make this the lab's default dev machine. At most one per lab. The only `@dev` machine in a lab is the default implicitly." |
      | "`workspace`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Host directory whose contents sync into the workspace, relative to the lab root. Without it the machine carries `@dev` but has nothing to sync." |
      | "`workspace_guest`" | "utf8" | "profile, else `/src`" | "Guest path the workspace lands at. Inherited from the profile (`C:\\src` on Windows profiles, `/src` on Linux ones, `/Users/Shared/src` on `macos`) if omitted." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Unset arguments resolve in the order `@dev` argument, then the machine's
    effective profile, then the vmlab floor of `/src`. A profile that sets no
    `workspace_guest` still hosts a dev machine. The schema rejects an unknown
    argument, a wrong type, a repeated `@dev`, or `@dev` on a block that is
    not a `vm` or `container`. Validation adds one rule: two machines with
    `default = true` is an error naming both. With more than one `@dev`
    machine and none declaring `default = true`, the lab has no default; the
    selection ladder in [vmlab dev](cmd_dev) then needs an argument. The
    `dev-container` example carries it on a container:
  DOC
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/dev-container/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/dev-container/vmlab.wcl"
    anchor      = "dev01"
    dedent      = true
  }

  h2 "login {}"
  p <<DOC
    A labelled identity on a machine: the guest account `exec`, `shell` and
    a script's `as_login` run as. See [Logins](guide_logins). Repeatable, so
    one account may be declared twice at different elevation, and `--user`
    selects between labels.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
login "<label>" {
  user     = "PROBE\\dev"
  password = "…"
  elevated = true
  default  = false
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`label`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Identity label, what `--user` and `as_login` select it by, for example `dev`. Unique per machine; the inline block label." |
      | "`user`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Guest account to log on as, for example `PROBE\\dev`." |
      | "`password`" | "utf8" | "none" | "The account's password, written plainly. Required on a Windows-family profile." |
      | "`elevated`" | "bool" | "`true`" | "Run the session elevated. Windows only; declaring it on a Linux-family or macOS-family profile is an error." |
      | "`default`" | "bool" | "implied" | "Make this the machine's default identity. Implied when the machine declares exactly one login." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The family the rules are judged against is the machine's resolved
    profile: a name starting with `windows` is Windows, one starting with
    `linux` is Linux, one starting with `macos` is macOS, and anything else, including `custom` and a registry
    template not yet pulled, is unknown and gets neither family rule. A
    container is always Linux. Validation enforces:
  DOC
  list {
    li "On a Windows family, every login has a `password`; the agent runs as LocalSystem and there is no credential-free logon."
    li "On a Linux or macOS family, no login declares `elevated`; root is root, and the field would be read nowhere. A login there needs no `password`: the agent is root."
    li "No two logins on one machine share a label."
    li "At most one login on a machine sets `default = true`; the message names both."
  }
  callout "The secret is in the lab file" { kind = :note body = <<DOC
    The password is written plainly because the lab's own provision script
    created the account with the same string. There is no credential store,
    no login verb and no wscript credential API.
  DOC
  }

  h2 "nic {}"
  p <<DOC
    One network interface, attached to a declared segment or to the lab's
    built-in NAT segment. A machine with no `nic {}` blocks has no network
    hardware at all. See [Networking](guide_networking).
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
nic {
  segment  = "corp"      // or: nat = true
  ip       = "10.50.0.10"
  gateway  = false
  mac      = "52:54:00:ab:cd:ef"
  isolated = false
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`segment`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Segment name to attach to. Required unless `nat = true`." |
      | "`nat`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Shorthand: attach to the per-lab built-in NAT segment." |
      | "`ip`" | "utf8" | "dynamic" | "Static IPv4, which becomes a DHCP reservation. Must be in the subnet and unique." |
      | "`gateway`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Make this NIC the segment gateway. It must own the subnet's first usable address." |
      | "`mac`" | "utf8" | "generated" | "Fixed MAC, for example `52:54:00:ab:cd:ef`. Generated and persisted otherwise." |
      | "`isolated`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Port isolation: reach the gateway and forwards but not segment neighbours." |
  }
  p "Validation enforces these rules:"
  list {
    li "Exactly one of `segment` or `nat = true` is set. Both is an error; neither is an error."
    li "`segment` names a segment declared in the lab."
    li "`ip` parses as an IPv4 address, lies inside the segment's declared subnet, is not the network, broadcast or gateway address, and is unique across the lab. A static IP needs a segment with a declared `subnet`, and is not supported on the built-in NAT segment."
    li "`mac` parses as a MAC address and is unique across the lab."
    li "`gateway = true` needs a declared segment, a static `ip` equal to the segment's first usable address, a segment without `nat = true`, a segment that is not `global`, and no other gateway NIC on that segment."
  }

  h2 "disk {}"
  p <<DOC
    An additional disk beyond the primary one: blank at a given size, or a
    fresh FAT filesystem with a folder copied onto it, or both.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
disk "data" { size = 10GiB }
disk "payload" { from = "./payload/" }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Disk identifier; the inline block label." |
      | "`size`" | "ByteSize" | "none" | "Blank disk size, for example `10GiB`. One of `size` or `from` is required." |
      | "`from`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Folder copied onto a fresh FAT filesystem. One of `size` or `from` is required." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    At least one of `size` and `from` must be set; both together is allowed.
    A `from` folder must exist under the lab root.
  DOC

  h2 "share {}"
  p <<DOC
    A host directory mounted into the guest, over virtiofs when host and
    guest support it, otherwise over SMB served at the segment gateway. See
    [Shared folders](guide_shares).
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
share {
  host      = "./shared"
  guest     = "/mnt/src"
  readonly  = false
  smb1      = false
  name      = "src"
  transport = "auto"
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`host`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Host directory to share. Must exist." |
      | "`guest`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Guest mount path, for example `/mnt/src` or `D:\\data`." |
      | "`readonly`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Mount read-only." |
      | "`smb1`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Enable the SMB1 dialect and the auth relaxation XP and 2003-era guests need." |
      | "`name`" | "utf8" | "derived" | "Share name. Derived from the guest path if omitted." |
      | "`transport`" | "utf8" | "`auto`" | "`auto` picks virtiofs when host and guest support it, else SMB; `virtiofs` or `smb` force one." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The derived name joins the alphanumeric runs of the guest path with
    underscores, so `/mnt/src` becomes `mnt_src` and `D:\\data` becomes
    `d_data`. Validation requires `host` to be a directory, a derivable or
    explicit `name`, and rejects `smb1 = true` with `transport = "virtiofs"`.
    A VM whose shares are not all `virtiofs` needs a NIC.
  DOC

  h2 "media {}"
  p <<DOC
    An ISO or floppy image built from a folder and attached to the machine.
    Built images are content-addressed under the lab's `.vmlab/` directory,
    so an unchanged folder is not rebuilt.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
media { kind = "iso" from = "./unattend/" label = "UNATTEND" }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`kind`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Image kind: `iso` or `floppy`." |
      | "`from`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Source folder built into the image. Must exist." |
      | "`label`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Volume label for the image." |
  }
  p "Validation requires `from` to be a directory under the lab root."

  h2 "gpu {}"
  p <<DOC
    GPU acceleration for the VM. At most one per VM. Passthrough hands a
    host device to the VM exclusively; virgl and vulkan render on the host
    GPU, which stays shared. The GPU itself is a host GPU, declared by name
    in the [host configuration file](ref_host): a PCI address is a fact about
    one machine, and the lab file travels between machines.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
gpu { mode = "vulkan" }                    // the host's default GPU
gpu { mode = "vulkan" host_gpu = "AMD1" }  // a named one
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`mode`" | "utf8" | "required" | "`passthrough`, `virgl` or `vulkan`." |
      | "`host_gpu`" | "utf8" | "the host's default GPU" | "Name of a host GPU declared in the host config, matched case-insensitively. Under `passthrough` the device handed to the VM; under `virgl` and `vulkan` the GPU that renders." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The lab daemon picks the host GPU when the VM starts. A `host_gpu` this
    host declares is used. A `host_gpu` it does not declare makes `virgl` and
    `vulkan` warn and take the host's default; `passthrough` refuses, naming
    the host's GPUs, because passing through a different card takes it away
    from the host. No `host_gpu` takes the default. When nothing resolves —
    no GPU declared, or the name missing and no default — `virgl` and
    `vulkan` render where QEMU puts them, and `passthrough` refuses.
    `vmlab status -v` shows the picked GPU as `host_gpu=<name>`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    A paravirtual GPU is the guest's only display adapter: `virtio-vga-gl` on
    x86 machines, `virtio-gpu-gl` with `-vga none` on `virt`. `vulkan` adds
    `venus=true,blob=true,hostmem=4G`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    vmlab reads the picked GPU's render node and kernel driver from
    `/sys/bus/pci/devices/<address>/`. The render node goes on the
    `egl-headless` display. Under `vulkan` the driver sets
    `VK_LOADER_DRIVERS_SELECT` in QEMU's environment: `*radeon*` for
    `amdgpu`, `*intel*` for `i915` and `xe`, `*nvidia*` for `nvidia`; a host
    GPU's `vulkan_icd` replaces that with `VK_DRIVER_FILES`. vmlab also loads
    its own implicit layer, `VK_LAYER_VMLAB_pci_filter`, through
    `VK_ADD_IMPLICIT_LAYER_PATH`, with `VMLAB_VK_PCI_ADDRESS` set to the
    GPU's address. The layer hides every other physical device, so the guest
    sees one Venus device even when two identical cards both match the
    driver filter. The other Vulkan loader variables are cleared, and
    `MESA_VK_DEVICE_SELECT` is never set, because it breaks Venus. `vmlab up` warns when a virgl or vulkan VM gets
    no host GPU and the host has more than one render node. Each run's
    `qemu.log` starts with the QEMU command line and the variables vmlab set.
    Under `passthrough` the address becomes `-device vfio-pci,host=<address>`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Validation runs the same pick: it rejects an empty `host_gpu`; a
    `passthrough` name this host lacks, or `passthrough` with nothing to pick;
    a picked GPU whose address names no PCI device, or no render node under
    virgl or vulkan; and a `vulkan_icd` that does not exist. A virgl or vulkan
    name this host lacks is a warning. The v0.24 fields `address` and
    `vulkan_icd` moved to the host config, and a lab still carrying either
    fails with the message in [Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting).
  DOC

  h2 "provision {}"
  p <<DOC
    A wscript provision script run on `vmlab up` once this machine is ready.
    It runs once, at its position among the machine's `provision` and
    `playbook` blocks. See [Guest automation with wscript](guide_scripting).
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
provision "scripts/setup.ws" { }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`script`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Path to the `.ws` file, relative to the lab root; the inline label. Must exist and compile." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Validation checks the file exists and compiles it, reporting compile
    errors against the block. Different machines provision in parallel; only
    `depends_on_provision` holds one machine's first step until another
    machine's steps have all finished.
  DOC

  h2 "playbook {}"
  p <<DOC
    A config-weave playbook applied on `vmlab up`, interleaved with the
    machine's provisions in declaration order, and runnable on demand with
    `vmlab playbook check` and `apply`. See [Playbooks](guide_playbooks).
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
playbook "playbooks/baseline" {
  play = "baseline"
  var "tz" { value = "UTC" }
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`path`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Playbook folder containing `playbook.wcl`, relative to the lab root; the inline label." |
      | "`play`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Play name inside the playbook to run." |
      | "`timeout`" | "duration" | "`1h`" | "Ceiling on each config-weave run, such as `2h` or `90min`. A run after a reboot gets the full value again." |
      | "`guest_dir`" | "utf8" | "profile's `playbook_dir`, else `/var/lib/vmlab/weave` (Linux, macOS) or `C:\\weave` (Windows)" | "Absolute guest directory the config-weave binary and the playbook folders land in. Created if missing." |
      | "`guest_bin`" | "utf8" | "unset" | "Absolute path of a config-weave binary already in the guest. When set, vmlab pushes no binary and probes this one with `version` before each run; the playbook folder is still pushed to `guest_dir`." |
      | "`var {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Variable overrides passed to config-weave for this machine's run." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Validation requires a non-empty `play`, a `path` that is a directory
    holding a `playbook.wcl`, a `timeout` greater than zero, and no variable
    set twice on one block. `guest_dir` and `guest_bin` must be absolute for
    the machine's guest family: a drive-letter path on Windows, a path
    starting with `/` on Linux (every container), and either where the
    family is unknown. On Windows `guest_bin` must also end in `.exe`. A
    macOS guest takes paths starting with `/`, and its playbook must name a
    `guest_bin`: config-weave ships no macOS binary for vmlab to push.
    config-weave ships guest binaries for x86_64 only, so a playbook on a VM
    with another known arch is rejected unless it names a `guest_bin`.
  DOC

  h2 "var {}"
  p <<DOC
    One variable override for the enclosing `playbook {}`, for this
    machine's run only. It reaches config-weave in a `--var-file` written to
    a private guest directory for the run, never on the guest command line.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
var "tz" { value = "UTC" }
var "join_password" { value = "Pa55w0rd!" secret = true }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Variable name; must be a WCL identifier. The inline block label." |
      | "`value`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Value, passed through verbatim. config-weave reads it as a WCL expression where it can (`3` is an int, `true` a bool) and as a string otherwise." |
      | "`secret`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Treat the value as a secret: it reaches config-weave in a separate `--secret-var-file`, which config-weave 0.12.2 and later scrub from their own output, and vmlab prints `***` in its place in run output, the lab log, `playbook.op.*` events, errors and `vmlab playbook list`. See [Playbooks](guide_playbooks)." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Validation requires the name to be letters, digits and underscores, not
    starting with a digit, because config-weave binds each override as a
    `let` inside the guest.
  DOC

  h2 "on {}"
  p <<DOC
    An event handler, declared inside `lab {}`. The handler script runs when
    the named event fires; a failing handler is logged and never fatal. See
    [Events and handlers](guide_events) and the [event list](ref_events).
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
on "vm.crashed" { run = "scripts/collect-dumps.ws" targets = ["dc01"] }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`event`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Event name to handle, for example `vm.crashed`; the inline block label." |
      | "`run`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Path to the handler `.ws` file, relative to the lab root. Must exist and compile." |
      | "`targets`" | "list<utf8>" | "none" | "VM or container names to restrict the handler to. Empty handles every occurrence." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Validation requires a known event name and a script that exists and
    compiles. `targets` may only be set on `vm.*`, `container.*` and
    `snapshot.*` events; a lab-wide event with targets is an error. A `vm.*`
    event may target only VMs and a `container.*` event only containers, and
    every target must exist.
  DOC
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/ad-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/ad-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    anchor      = "handlers"
    dedent      = true
  }
}
